rRaven/Raven_et_al._2025_Fisheries_Research.git","description":"Spatial and temporal stability of tributary-specific otolith trace element signatures","navbar":{"left":[{"href":"index.qmd","text":"Overview"}],"right":[{"icon":"github","href":"https://github.com/OlivierRaven/Raven_et_al._2025_Fisheries_Research.git"}],"logo":{}}},"format":{"html":{"theme":"cosmo","toc":true,"toc-depth":3,"toc-location":"right","number-sections":true,"code-fold":true,"code-tools":true,"fig-cap-location":"bottom","link-external-newwindow":true}},"theme":"cosmo","toc-location":"right","fig-cap-location":"bottom","title":"Assessment of spatial and temporal stability of tributary-specific otolith trace element signatures","subtitle":"Brown Trout (*Salmo trutta*) in the Lower Clutha River Catchment","author":[{"name":"O.V. Raven","affiliations":[{"name":"University of Otago","department":"Department of Zoology","city":"Dunedin","country":"New Zealand"},{"name":"Wageningen University & Research","department":"Aquaculture & Fisheries Group","city":"Wageningen","country":"The Netherlands"}],"email":"olivier.raven@icloud.com","corresponding":true},{"name":"J.E.M. Couper","affiliations":[{"name":"Fish & Game Otago","city":"Dunedin","country":"New Zealand"}]},{"name":"H. Trotter","affiliations":[{"name":"Fish & Game Otago","city":"Dunedin","country":"New Zealand"}]},{"name":"M.R. Reid","affiliations":[{"name":"University of Otago","department":"Department of Geology","city":"Dunedin","country":"New Zealand"}]},{"name":"R. Svirgsden","affiliations":[{"name":"University of Tartu","department":"Estonian Marine Institute","city":"Tartu","country":"Estonia"}]},{"name":"M. Rohtla","affiliations":[{"name":"University of Tartu","department":"Estonian Marine Institute","city":"Tartu","country":"Estonia"}]},{"name":"G.P. Closs","affiliations":[{"name":"University of Otago","department":"Department of Zoology","city":"Dunedin","country":"New Zealand"}]}],"date":"April 2025","abstract":"Otolith trace element analysis is a powerful tool for studying fish migration between juvenile and adult habitats. To track catchment-scale migration, trace element signatures in juvenile otoliths are compared to the otolith natal region of adults, with the aim of matching adults to their natal habitat. However, the stability of signatures in natal habitat over time, crucial for this method to work across generations, has rarely been tested.\n\nTo evaluate spatiotemporal stability of natal stream trace element signatures in otoliths, a four-year study in New Zealand's lower Clutha River/Mata-Au was executed. 602 brown trout juveniles from 20 tributaries were sampled and trace element analysis was performed, enabling comparisons of the signatures within each stream over time.\n\nThe findings suggest the signatures of the elements Mn, Sr, and Ba incorporated into otoliths are generally stable within each catchment over four-years. This means that, if tracking adult trout back to their natal stream it is not necessary to analyse juvenile otoliths from each year. Spatial variability of Mn, Sr and Ba was tested by using Random Forest and Linear Discriminant Analysis models, which assigned juvenile trout to the correct stream in 61% and 52% cases, respectively. Grouping the sites using Nearest-Neighbour Cluster Analysis resulted in 93% accuracy for both models. Collection of juveniles from key spawning streams across 2-3 years should be sufficient to establish reference signatures for each tributary that will facilitate tracking of adult migration over several years.\n","keywords":["Brown trout","Otolith trace element analysis","Otolith","Salmo trutta","Spatial variability","Temporal stability"],"funding":"This research was funded by Contact Energy Ltd","citation":{"container-title":"Fisheries Research"}},"extensions":{"book":{"multiFile":true}}}}